text:
sanjay: Follow-up to the authz cleanup RFC: proposing a small policy runtime that we can run as a sidecar or embed. goal: remove copy-pasted checks across console render paths and make rule rollout auditable.
emma: I like single source of truth. main worry is added hop cost on hot paths. what's the plan for keeping p99 tight?
li: thinking hybrid: an in-process fast cache of evaluated decisions (very compact) for hot keys, plus a sidecar / svc that holds canonical rules and does full eval. cache entries should be tiny and versioned.
sanjay: exact plan: policy runtime exposes a tiny gRPC Decide endpoint. console instances try local cache first; on miss they call sidecar. cache entries carry policy_version and a small signature so we can detect staleness. target local-hit rate > 95% for typical dashboards.
karen: for on-prem/private installs we must support embedding the runtime natively (no network). maybe compile rules to a WASM blob and load into the console process; that keeps determinism and low-latency.
li: WASM approach sounds promising; fast, sandboxed. keep the rule language declarative (PEL - policy expression language) with only pure predicates and simple set ops so evaluation time is bounded. no loops, no external calls.
emma: what's a realistic TTL for cache entries? too long risks incorrect denies/allow if policy changes.
sanjay: propose dual-metric approach: short soft-TTL for automatic eviction (3s) and a policy_version tag that causes immediate invalidation when management plane pushes a new version. so most runtime misses are transient; management pushes bump version triggering evictions.
infra-bot: infra-bot: reminder: any sidecar increases failure modes. require multi-zone replicas for sidecar + client-side circuit breakers and a safe fallback mode. :rotating_light:
karen: fallback UX needs product decisions. for sensitive routes we should degrade to deny when policy service is unreachable; for low-risk views degrade to allow but mark as stale and emit an audit event.
li: audit correlation is important: include stable decision_id, policy_version, and evaluation_latency in logs so SRE + Product can tie incidents to changes.
emma: can we see an example contract? prefer gRPC proto sketch.
sanjay: rough proto: 
```
service PolicyService {
 rpc Decide(DecisionRequest) returns (DecisionReply) {}
}
message DecisionRequest {
 string tenant = 1;
 string subject_id = 2;
 string route = 3;
 map<string,string> attributes = 4; // e.g. token_scopes, panel_owner
 string trace_id = 5; // for correlation
}
message DecisionReply {
 enum Outcome { ALLOW = 0; DENY = 1; CONDITIONAL = 2; }
 Outcome outcome = 1;
 string decision_id = 2;
 string policy_version = 3;
 uint32 cache_lifespan_ms = 4;
 string reason = 5;
}
```
li: include conditional for cases where sidecar suggests further inline checks (e.g., rate-limit or field-level masking). good.
karen: ops note: sidecar should expose metrics: local_cache_hit, remote_calls, avg_latency_ms, stale_policies_seen. add a dashboard + SLA for decision service.
sanjay: perf tests: (A) baseline: current inline checks; (B) hybrid with in-process cache hit; (C) cold-miss path hitting sidecar. measure p50/p95/p99/p999 and CPU/mem per instance. simulate tenancy + burst startup.
emma: also add an offline validation step in the management API so rule authors can run a dry-run against sample contexts to catch surprises.
li: cost model: estimate memory per cached-entry ~ 200 bytes (key + small decision blob). at 10k concurrent panels per instance worst-case that's ~2MB unique keys, so still small. but compiled WASM policies might add ~5-10MB per tenant if embedded - need to measure.
karen: security: signed policy bundles and integrity checks when loading embedded blobs. for private installs we must publish clear commands to rotate keys and push new bundles.
sanjay: proposed rollout plan: (1) build sidecar + in-process cache shim, (2) canary on low-risk routes with default-allow degrade, (3) promote and enable default-deny on sensitive routes once coverage & perf looks good, (4) add WASM embed option for private installs.
emma: action items: sanjay: prototype runtime + canary on dev org; li: memory & perf model; karen: runbook + golden metrics + HA checklist; me: product policy for degrade defaults per-route.
li: I'll add perf harness and a synthetic tenant generator. aim to share numbers next week.
infra-bot: infra-bot: created ticket INFRA-1987 for sidecar HA + circuit-breaker.
sanjay: thanks, will start drafting the RFC follow-up with proto + perf plan. :thumbsup:
